==Usage== <!--T:5-->

<!--T:6-->
# Select one or more objects or sub-elements in the 3D view
# Run the macro from {{MenuCommand|Macro → Macros → Get Global Placement → Execute}}
# The results will appear in the {{MenuCommand|View → Panels → Report view}} panel
# For each selected object, the output includes:

===Output Format=== <!--T:7-->

<!--T:8-->
The Report View shows the global placement in the following format:
{{Code|code=
=== Object Name ===
Placement: [X, Y, Z, Rotation Axis: (rx, ry, rz), Angle: deg]
Global Scale: [sx, sy, sz]
}}
* **Position (X, Y, Z)**: World coordinates of the object's origin
* **Rotation**: Rotation axis and angle in degrees
* **Scale**: Global scale factor, accounting for link scale transformations

===Example=== <!--T:9-->

<!--T:10-->
For an object nested inside a Part container with a placement of {{incode|App.Placement(App.Vector(100,0,0), App.Rotation()}} and inside a link with scale {{incode|2.0}}, the macro will output:
{{Code|code=
=== MyPart.MyObject ===
Placement: [200.00, 0.00, 0.00, Rotation Axis: (0.00, 0.00, 1.00), Angle: 0.00°]
Global Scale: [2.00, 2.00, 2.00]
}}

==Notes== <!--T:11-->

<!--T:12-->
* Works with all object types, including bodies, sketches, and datum geometry
* Fully supports nested link arrays (App::LinkArray, App::LinkGroup)
* The macro does NOT modify any object — it only reads and displays placement data
* Results are read-only and output to the Report View; copy them from there as needed
* If nothing appears in the Report View, ensure it is enabled: {{MenuCommand|View → Panels → Report view}}

